
== §1 — MAIN.JS IS THE ONE ENTRY, AND IT IMPORTS EVERY MODULE EXACTLY ONCE ==
  ✓ the entry module exists
  ✓ the module set is not empty — this suite would otherwise be vacuous
  ✓ main.js carries no logic: every top-level statement is an import
  ✓ every main.js import is a bare side-effect import of a relative module
  ✓ the reader agrees with the parser on the import order
  ✓ no module is imported twice by main.js
  ✓ every module main.js imports really exists on disk
  ✓ not one module under public/app is orphaned — every non-boot, non-style module is either imported by main.js or declared lazy
  ✓ main.js imports nothing beyond those modules
  ✓ the declared lazy set and the eager order are disjoint — no module is both shipped and deferred
  ✓ every declared lazy module really exists on disk
  ✓ the lazy declaration is not vacuous — it defers real modules
  ✓ the two leaf registries are imported first, in the declared order

== §2 — THE FIRST-PARTY IMPORT GRAPH IS ACYCLIC ==
  ✓ every module parsed
  ✓ the graph is not empty — this suite would otherwise be vacuous
  ✓ every import target resolves to a real module under public/app
  ✓ the only bare specifiers are the three renderer entries declared in the import map
  ✓ there is no import cycle anywhere in public/app
  ✓ the traversal really reached every module (it did not stop early)

== §3 — EVERY EDGE POINTS BACKWARDS IN MAIN.JS IMPORT ORDER ==
  ✓ no module imports one that is evaluated later or at the same rank in the combined (eager then lazy) evaluation order
  ✓ not one eager module — main.js included — statically imports a declared lazy module; otherwise the deferral is undone without a trace
  ✓ main.js itself imports nothing but graph modules (its own edges are the entry edges, not intra-graph edges)
  ✓ the combined evaluation order is a valid topological order of the whole graph

== §4 — NO UNRESOLVED FREE IDENTIFIER IN ANY MODULE ==
  ✓ every file under public/app was scanned
  ✓ not one module reaches for a name that is neither imported, nor declared, nor a declared browser global
  ✓ the declared-globals list is the real one and is not empty
  ✓ the two named exemptions stay exactly two — no quiet growth

== §5 — __ACS_LATE IS READ EARLY AND WRITTEN ONCE, BY ITS OWNER ==
  ✓ the registry is a sealed object literal, so an unregistered name cannot be published by accident
  ✓ the registry declares the twenty-two forward references it is documented to (twenty values + two live references)
  ✓ the two names that are reassigned after publish are ACCESSORS, not value slots — a value slot is exactly the snapshot bug
  ✓ and each accessor has a live-reference slot beside it
  · live registry witness NOT VERIFIED here (no bundle scope)
  ✓ no module writes into __ACS_LATE by any route other than its one Object.assign publish line
  ✓ every registry key is published by exactly one module
  ✓ the published names are exactly the registry keys — no key is dead and no name is published without a slot
  ✓ the registry is actually used — this section would otherwise be vacuous
  ✓ every __ACS_LATE name a module reads is really published somewhere
  ✓ a module only ever reads a __ACS_LATE name whose owner the combined order evaluates strictly earlier — which is exactly why the edge was moved into the registry instead of becoming a forward import
  ✓ no __ACS_LATE name is read while a module is being evaluated — every read sits inside a function, so the registry is full by call time

== §6 — EVERY DECLARED LAZY MODULE IS FETCHED BY EXACTLY ONE DYNAMIC IMPORT, FROM THE PANEL ENTRY ALONE ==
  ✓ every dynamic import specifier is a static string — nothing is computed at runtime, so this graph is knowable at build time
  ✓ every declared lazy module is the target of at least one dynamic import
  ✓ no lazy module is fetched from two places — one call site, one owner
  ✓ the panel entry is the only module that fetches a lazy layer
  ✓ the panel entry fetches nothing but declared lazy modules — it is an entry point, not a general loader

